	The original post was on Making a 3d version of hack,  That is what
we are technically discussing.  

1:	the proposal is fine
2:	thier are problems
	a:	the name will need be different I suggest Hack3d
	b: 	the graphics interface will be machine specific
		1)  this can be slightly alieviated by using X on
		    most platforms save the PC version  (more on that)
		2)  a binary image will be needed for each creature :(
		    this will consume MAJOR disk space.  and require
		    a universal image format be used
		3)  All unix versions (save linux) should/must use X
		    otherwise NO consistancy will be possible :(
	c:	Sound will have to be added AFTER the initial release
		(sound is very machine dependant and a royal pain)
3:	converting existing code
	a:	Not to hard since NH is a BLOCK oreinted environment
		this means that "we" can treat the universe like 
		a BIG wolf3d level (grin)
	b:	Actions and special effects will need to change some
		BUT believe it or not this is viable.  
		1)  picking up stuff will have to be changed a bit
		    but essentially remain the same a PICKLIST 
		2)  keyborad interface changed (directionally oreinted)
		    since NH is not "real" time (IE you can piddle around
		    b4 you make a move) I suggest a PAUSE ability so you 
		    can do whatever thinking you need.  
	        3)  for simplicity a wolf 3d scrolling interface is not
		    neccessary.  You could in fact make each "step" or 
		    movement between "blocks" rigid or incremental without
		    scrificing to much.  (You could still have creatures
		    move each time you do)
		4)  have an overhead view of the scene.  IE Each time you
		    move you have a map you are viewing at the same time.
	c:	line of sight will not need much change and in fact the
		same code can be used since (grin) you will need it for
		viewing a distance.  Remember a persistant level screen
		shown somewhere will keep things sane. :)

I think it's possible to do, it will require some incremental work.
I suggest starting SIMPLE and not trying to put everything in all
at once.  This way you will at least have the basics working for 
experimentation.  For example I would put in a simple 3d interface
that's not real time to start with IE each move is a game tick.
A different keyboard interface ( so you can "look" around).  Simple
bitmaps for each CLASS (instead of bitmaps for each TYPE) of monster.
No sound.  No special FX (IE flickering tourches etc).  Just a nice RAW
base.  This will allow the initial version to be as bug freeas possible.

This user interfaceis the MOST important thing to work on first.  The
other "neat" stuff can wait.  Nethack is more like chess than a real
time game.  


